              DIGITAL ANNOUNCES RECIPIENTS OF WORLDWIDE
                   CHILDREN'S CONTRIBUTION PROGRAM


MAYNARD, Mass. -- June 2, 1995 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced the 82 recipients of its Children's Contribution 
Program, a $170,000 Request for Proposal (RFP) process that provides 
individual grants ranging from $500 to $5,000.  Through the RFP, the 
company invests charitable resources in local community 
organizations around the world that provide programs to children and 
youth, primarily in the areas of education, health and human 
services.

     The Children's Contribution Program, funded through Digital's 
Corporate Contributions Program office, awarded the grants based on 
the organization's ability to provide a valuable community service 
that impacts a significant number of children.  A sampling of the 
organizations that received grants includes: 

     o Save the Children Canada, Toronto
     o Chernobyl Children's Homeopathic Fund, Ireland
     o AL-SAM, Anti Drug Abuse Association, Israel
     o Sung Ro Won Babies Home, Korea
     o Ukrainian Youth Association, Kiev
     o Make A Wish Foundation of America (MAW), Arizona
     o Colorado AIDS Project (CAP)
     o Youth Service Project, Chicago
     o Children's Home Society, Minnesota
     o Dimock Community Health Center, Roxbury
     o Girls, Inc., Nashua
     o Caring Program for Children, Pittsburgh.  

     According to Jane Hamel, manager of Digital's Corporate 
Contributions Program, "Our investment in these community programs 
reflects the company's interest in supporting opportunities for 
children to grow, to be healthy, and to succeed.  We are gratified 
by the response we received and are pleased with the range of 
services and geographic scope represented by the recipient 
organizations."

     Digital's Children's Contribution Program is part of the 
company's charitable focus on investing in children and youth around 
the world.  "Digital believes that children deserve to have positive 
experiences in their lives and access to choices that will encourage 
them to reach their full potential.  The company invests its 
charitable resources in programs that provide opportunities for 
children and young adults to learn, to enjoy a sense of well-being, 
and to thrive in an environment of achievement," stated Hamel.

