             ...Worldwide User Group Forms in Dallas...  

  
      DIGITAL ANNOUNCES TeMIP V.3, THE FIRST FULLY DISTRIBUTED
  NETWORK MANAGEMENT FRAMEWORK FOR THE TELECOMMUNICATIONS INDUSTRY


DALLAS, Texas -- June 8, 1995 -- Digital Equipment Corporation today 
announced network management software that meets the growing need of  
telecommunications service providers to manage distributed, 
multivendor networks.  The distributed capabilities are included in 
Digital's TeMIP V.3 (Telecommunications Management Information 
Platform), announced here today at the first worldwide TeMIP user 
group meeting.  

     TeMIP software provides network managers with a unified view 
from which to control diverse elements of public switched 
communications networks, including cellular and conventional 
telephone, broadcast and cable television, and wireless and wireline 
networks.  TeMIP V.3 extends this functionality with distributed 
network management capability.  This provides network managers with 
unprecedented performance, flexibility and scalabilty in managing  
networks of hundreds of thousands of network elements. 

     "Advancing transmission and multimedia technology are driving a 
worldwide need for distributed network management solutions which 
are both robust and flexible. ATM, SONET and its European equivalent 
SDH, for example, are raising new network management challenges for 
public carriers, much like the new demands placed on cable and 
broadcast providers by the integration of ISDN and Video on Demand," 
said Jean Claude Sainctavit, Digital's vice president for the 
telecommunications industry.  "Today's announcement reaffirms Digital's 
commitment to lead the industry in providing managers of large 
telecommunications networks with the information technology 
infrastructure they need to remain competitive and deliver quality 
services to their customers." 

     Since its introduction in 1992, as the leading network 
management solution designed specifically for the telecommunications 
industry, more than 20 of the world's largest telecommunications 
operators have implemented Digital's TeMIP software.  With TeMIP 
V.3, Digital becomes the first company to provide fully distributed, 
network management that meets telecommunications standards for 
multivendor, distributed computing environments.   New with TeMIP 
V.3 are the following features:  

     *	Distributed Network Management.  
     
     	TeMIP software now has inter-manager communication 
        mechanisms that provide transparent, remote operation in 
        distributed environments.  This extends the management 
     	environment from isolated units in which 20 or more 
        managers could service some 50,000 network devices to an 
        integrated system in which hundreds of managers control 
        hundreds of thousands of network devices.  Each new 
        management node on the network boosts the number of 
        management events that can be processed each second.  By 
        coupling assigned access rights to applications across 
        the entire network, TeMIP increases productivity. 

     	A three-tiered distribution strategy separates system 
        access, applications and user information so that each 
        can be deployed for maximum productivity benefit.  To 
        improve customer service, for example, a public carrier 
        might extend network management access to a customer. The 
        system is also fully scalable, such that customers can 
        add applications and users to the system transparently, 
        as network management requirements grow. 
 
     *	Improved Graphical User Interface (GUI).  
   
     	The TeMIP desktop has a friendlier look and feel about 
        it, due to new backdrop and icon formats, simpler 
        navigation support and toolbars, multiple screen support, 
        and integration with configuration and fault management 
        applications.  
   
     *	Improved alarm handling. 
  
     	A new Low Level Filtering and Correlation feature 
        prevents unnecessary event/alarm information from passing 
        through the system.  This speeds up problem detection and 
        diagnosis, while freeing operators to address the most 
        pressing problems, such as network failures.   
 
     Other features of TeMIP V.3 include improvements to the SNMP 
management that was introduced in V.2 and new alarms management and 
trouble ticket applications. 

     "As cable companies, new wireless and wireline operators, and 
the traditional public carriers strategize to offer new 
technological solutions to the end user, Digital is stepping up to 
provide the communications industry with a management product that 
integrates old and new networking technologies, lowers costs, and 
improves availability and reliability," said Sylvia Clark, senior 
analyst with Boston-based Aberdeen Group.  "Paired with Digital's 
Alpha platform, with unparalleled speed and an immense flat memory 
profile, TeMIP should really impress the industry."

     In addition to the growing customer base that is represented at 
the Dallas TeMIP user group, there is the growing interest among 
third party telecommunications integration service providers.  
Flemming Buccholt, Managing Director of Netman A/S, for example,    
attributes much of the rapid growth of his 50 person systems 
integration firm to the functionality of TeMIP which enabled him to 
build, deliver and invoice advanced Telecommunications Management 
Network (TMN) solutions quickly.  Buccholt is very interested in the 
potential of TeMIP V.3 because of the increasing demand he is seeing   
for distributed network management.  

     Likewise  Dr. Kehne, operator of the Dusseldorf-based PCS 
Network E-Plus, partnered with Digital in network management because 
of Digital's experience in the telecommunications market.  Due to 
its flexibility and scalability, Dr. Kehne is looking to TeMIP V.3 
to provide greater support in coping with "tough real-time 
requirements and handling of very high data volumes."   

     TeMIP complies with OMNIPoint specifications, ISO10164-x and 
10165-x  management standards, as well as ITU-T X.73x and M.3010 
Telecommunications Management Network (TMN) recommendations. Also 
supported are DME-compliant X/Open Management Protocol (XMP) 
application programming interfaces.  New service elements, 
transmission nodes and other devices -- as well as management 
capabilities -- can easily be added without disrupting existing 
operations and with guaranteed investment protection.   

     TeMIP delivers a robust, network-proven set of applications -- 
a full range of alarm management capabilities, event logging (which 
supports the seven classes of ISO-defined log records) -- and is one 
of the first commercially available trouble-tracking systems 
designed from the OMNIPoint 1 Forum 024 and T1.227/228 trouble 
report standards.  TeMIP guarantees the high level of security 
essential to network operations with its flexible and powerful 
access control mechanism and audit trail. 

     TeMIP software runs on the Digital UNIX(R) platform, and can 
manage networks on all leading network operating systems used in the 
telecommunications industry and in the largest corporate voice/data 
networks.  TeMIP software is fully interoperable with Digital's 
POLYCENTER NetView network management software, which is Digital's 
strategic solution for managing data networks in open enterprise 
client/server environments.   

     TeMIP V.3  pricing depends upon the configuration.  A single 
standalone system including the distribution framework and alarm 
management and TroubleTicket applications would cost approximately 
$100,000.  Shipments begin in September, 1995.  

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry- 
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace.
