             DIGITAL ANNOUNCES THREE-TIERED STRATEGY FOR
           DIGITAL MEDIA STUDIO'S INTERACTIVE CONTENT AND
                  APPLICATION DEVELOPMENT BUSINESS


    Studio Ramps Up with Enhanced MPEG-2 Compression Technology;
                 Hires Cable and Multimedia Experts


MAYNARD, Mass. -- June 7, 1995 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced it is pursuing a three-tiered strategy for its 
Digital Media Studio to become the industry-leading provider of the 
critical resources required to design, develop, and release 
interactive applications for distribution over multiple broadband 
networks.

     As part of its strategy, the Digital Media Studio will engage 
in joint production alliances with content developers to facilitate 
the creation of interactive applications; offer complete program 
development services spanning a variety of production capabilities, 
from storyboarding to digitally converting applications for seamless 
integration within the interactive environment; and provide digital 
encoding services that convert massive amounts of content into 
broadcast-quality, interactive programming.

     "The Studio's three-tiered strategy is directed along two 
business models -- functioning as a contracted service provider, as 
in the case of our MPEG encoding business, and as a full 
collaborator that publishes co-developed content on multiple 
networks," said Jim Turner, general manager and executive producer 
of the Digital Media Studio. "To further this plan, we have hired 
talented, seasoned personnel enabling us to provide top-notch expertise 
for the complete design and development of interactive video 
applications.  What's more, we now have the capability to quickly 
convert large amounts of video programming to high-quality, digital 
format via the MPEG-2 compression process.  These enhancements will 
enable us to effectively help customers develop content that drives 
consumer demand for interactive video services."

     The studio now provides full, MPEG-2 (Moving Picture Experts 
Group) encoding for high-performance, broadcast-quality video.  It 
also expanded its staff with key industry professionals, including 
Kathleen Held, formerly of MTV Networks, as senior producer 
responsible for spearheading all programming efforts; and James 
Kimak, previously of Prodigy, as senior graphic designer responsible 
for forging the studio's creative direction and overseeing the 
design of interactive applications.

Compression Technology Upgraded

     Digital Media Studio's MPEG-2 encoding services are optimized 
for high-volume projects, typical of interactive applications in 
such markets as entertainment, retail, education, health care, 
banking and financial services.  In addition, the Studio's MPEG-2 
capability and state-of-the-art video preprocessing ensure the 
broadcast quality necessary for mass market interactive 
applications.  MPEG-2, specially designed for broadcast video, 
creates a more lifelike video product: This is because MPEG-2 can 
encode at higher resolutions and frame rates than MPEG-1.

     In addition to digital encoding of both MPEG-1 and MPEG-2, the 
Digital Media Studio offers complete production services, including 
concept development; storyboarding; creation and conversion of 
audio, video, and graphics to support multiple platforms; 
programming; integration; testing and production management.  
Content developed through the Studio is also compatible with Digital 
Equipment's Mediaplex(superscript: tm) server infrastructure, now 
involved in more trials and deployments worldwide than any other 
server.

Senior Personnel Hired from Cable and Multimedia Industries

     As senior producer, Kathleen Held will oversee the management 
and production of interactive programming.  She will work closely 
with content developers who are creating interactive applications in 
such markets as entertainment, banking and financial services, news, 
retail, education and health care.  Held was previously director of 
production, On-Air Promos, at "HA! The TV Comedy Network" (now known 
as Comedy Central), an MTV Network.  Held has 15 years of experience 
in television production, having produced and directed programs in 
entertainment, sports, fashion and travel.  She holds an 
undergraduate degree from the University of Michigan.

     James Kimak has been hired as senior graphic designer, leading 
the overall art direction of interactive content and applications 
created at the studio.  Kimak has extensive experience in on-line 
art direction and design, having previously served as editorial 
design manager at Prodigy.  His background also includes newspaper 
and publication illustration, and he is an exhibiting fine art 
painter.  He holds an undergraduate degree from University of 
Bridgeport.

     Digital Equipment's Video and Interactive Information Services 
group established the Digital Media Studio in Tarrytown, New York, 
as part of its strategy to drive the demand for interactive 
information services by assisting and accelerating interactive 
content development.  The Tarrytown facility is the first of a 
worldwide network of Digital Media Studios.  It is an important 
element in the company's full-service offerings for video service 
providers and content developers.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digitals scalable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in todays global marketplace.
