                   DIGITAL AND CANADIAN GOVERNMENT
             INVEST $15 MILLION (CANADIAN) TO ESTABLISH
                    NATIONAL MULTIMEDIA INSTITUTE
                 FOR INTERACTIVE CONTENT DEVELOPMENT


      Institute to Provide Complete Services for Designing and
   Developing Interactive Video Services; Key Element of Digital's
                      Content Creation Strategy


MAYNARD, Mass. -- June 14, 1995 --  Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced the foundation of the National Multimedia Institute 
(NMI), to offer Canadian-based content providers, organizations, and 
governments a variety of integrated solutions for developing 
interactive media.  The National Multimedia Institute, located 
within the Canadian Museum of Civilization in Hull, Quebec, will be 
a complete center for the design, development, storage, and 
distribution of multimedia applications, information, and solutions 
over multiple, broadband networks and the Internet.  It has been 
established with $3.25 million (Canadian) in start-up subsidy funds 
from the Canadian and Quebec governments, and $11.75 million 
(Canadian) to be provided by Digital and its partners over the first 
three years.

     NMI will establish alliances and partnerships with key 
companies to develop full-service offerings, making it Canada's only 
facility providing all components of this converging environment. 
NMI will bring together the resources required for the creation, 
storage, distribution, testing, and marketing of interactive 
multimedia applications, as well as training of content development 
professionals. To simplify the use of interactive applications for 
consumers, NMI will conduct research on human interfaces for multimedia 
with colleges, universities, and other companies.

     "In addition to distance learning and business-based multimedia 
solutions -- which are two key application areas in Canada -- home 
shopping, banking and video-on-demand are other interactive 
applications being explored," said Sultan Zia, vice president of 
Digital's Video and Interactive Information Services group.  "We are 
pleased to be working together with the Canadian and Quebec 
governments on this foundational element of Canada's technology 
infrastructure."

     Digital is establishing an international network of multimedia 
content development centers to accelerate the creation of 
interactive video programming, spark consumer purchase of 
interactive services, and ultimately increase demand for interactive 
technology.  The company opened its first center, the Digital Media 
Studio in Tarrytown, New York, last November.  

