              DIGITAL SIGNALS BREAKTHROUGH PERFORMANCE
         OF ENTERPRISE SERVERS, SUPPORT FOR ORACLE WAREHOUSE


NEW YORK, NY -- June 21, 1995 -- Digital Equipment Corporation today 
announced estimated performance results for its AlphaServer 8400 
enterprise servers, further demonstrating the power and price/ 
performance advantages of Digital's AlphaGeneration products.

     Digital also announced new developments in its marketing and 
technology partnership with leading database vendor Oracle 
Corporation, of Redwood Shores, CA, including support for Oracle 
Warehouse, a comprehensive solution for designing and implementing 
data warehouse and decision support systems. 

     Digital announced these developments at the Gartner Group 
Commercial Parallel Processing Technology Briefing, where Digital 
and other leading vendors joined Gartner in a panel discussion on 
trends in commercial parallel processing. 

Breakthrough Performance Estimates

     Working closely with Oracle, in April Digital delivered 
breakthrough performance, scalability, and affordability by 
introducing its 64-bit AlphaServer 8400 and 8200 symmetric 
multiprocessing (SMP) servers. Digital today said preliminary 
testing of the AlphaServer 8400 has shown an estimated 9,000 
transactions per minute (TPM). 

     According to Pauline Nist, Digital Vice President of 
AlphaServer Business Segment, "We promised breathtaking performance 
on the AlphaServer 8000 platform. Now we are delivering on this 
promise, and further corroborating benchmark results customers and 
partners, such as Oracle, are generating."

     In April, Oracle introduced its unique 64-bit Very Large Memory 
option to the industry-leading Oracle7 database. The 64-bit option 
is available only on AlphaServer 8000 systems. It enables businesses 
to run the database portion of applications, such as data 
warehousing, video-on-demand, and online transaction processing, 
more than 200 times faster than on current 32-bit systems. 

     Analysts are bullish on the AlphaServer 8000 line. "Alpha's 
offensive on the commercial multiprocessing server market, once the 
domain of mainframes, has clearly established a solid beachhead," 
said Terry C. Shannon, a research analyst at Illuminata, a computer 
technology assessment firm in Hollis, NH. "The new AlphaServer 8000 
series machines look particularly capable of capturing significant 
share in this growing market."

     According to Stephen Brobst, Managing Partner, Strategic 
Technologies & Systems, "The AlphaServer 8000 entry into the 
marketplace has clearly raised the SMP ceiling. This will enable 
many high-end customers to implement low risk SMP technology without 
the need to move to less mature MPP architectures."  

     In all, Digital has shipped more than 100,000 Alpha systems 
since introducing the world's fastest servers and workstations in 
1992. Revenue from Alpha systems has surpassed $3 billion. "At this 
rate," said Nist, "we expect to have reached $4.5 billion worth of 
Alpha systems before our major competitors -- IBM, HP, Sun -- ship 
their first 64-bit systems."

Oracle Warehouse Support

     Broadening its strategic relationship with database leader 
Oracle Corporation, Digital is investing significant resources in 
supporting Oracle Warehouse, announced by Oracle today, to provide a 
full spectrum of consulting, architecture and design, and 
development services to ensure successful implementation of data 
warehouse pilots.

     "Oracle Warehouse offers customers a comprehensive set of 
services for implementing data warehouse and decision support 
systems," said Nist. "Working closely with Oracle, Digital is proud 
to provide not only the most powerful data warehouse engine, the 
AlphaServer 8000 family of SMP servers, but also a full spectrum of 
data warehouse services." 

     "Digital and Oracle have teamed to provide robust, secure, 
scaleable data warehouse solutions for years," said David Puglia, 
Oracle Director of Product Marketing, "Customers such as 
Ingersoll-Rand, ITT-Hartford Life Insurance, Shared Medical Systems, 
REECO, a Division of EG&G, and New England Electric have deployed 
data warehouses on the Digital platform during the last 18 months 
alone." 

     Digital recently beat IBM SP2 and Sequent multiprocessing 
servers to capture more than $1 million in business at Rhone Poulenc 
Rorer, a Philadelphia-based pharmaceutical company. A four-CPU 
AlphaServer 8400 system, with 2GB of memory running the Oracle 
64-bit option, will enable RPR to run a 200GB data warehousing 
application. 

     "As we begin implementing a data warehouse solution for 
strategic field force targeting, we realize that we're going to have 
a very large database, in the hundreds of gigabytes," said Jim 
Manousos, Director of Sales and Marketing Information Systems. "It's 
important that we're able to load a lot of that database into 
memory.  With the Digital-Oracle solution, we have the right 
answer."

Broad Global Reach

     New accounts from all industries, as well as current customers 
worldwide, are investing in AlphaServer 8000 breakthrough solutions. 
"From telecommunications and health care, to financial investing, 
retail sales, and manufacturing, commercial and scientific 
enterprises worldwide are moving to AlphsServer 8000 systems," said 
David A. Poole, Director of the Enterprise Server Product Line. 
These include:

     Baxter Healthcare,USA 
     Centre-File, Ltd., London 
     Donnelley Marketing, Inc., USA 
     Maruti Udyog, Ltd., India 
     Mitel Corporation, Canada 
     Nanyang Technological University, Singapore 
     Sofrecom, a subsidiary of France Telecom 
     Swiss Telecom PTT 
     Telecom South Africa 
     University of Mainz, Germany

     In addition, leading software from partners such as Dun & 
Bradstreet Software Services, Information Builders, Inc., Red Brick 
Systems, SAP AG, SAS Institute, and more than 1,500 others offer 
applications and tools for AlphaServer 8000 systems.

     Meanwhile, leading channel partners, such as Pioneer Standard, 
Avnet Computer, and Wyle are seeing widespread growth in demand for 
the new Digital SMP servers. 

AlphaServer 8000 Systems

     Featuring the most advanced 64-bit RISC SMP technology, 
AlphaServer 8400 5/300 and AlphaServer 8200 5/300 enterprise servers 
are the world's fastest servers, based on the industry's fastest 
chip, the world's first BIPS (billions of instructions per second) 
microprocessor -- the Alpha 21164.  

     For commercial applications, the AlphaServer 8400 server, with 
up to 12 CPUs and 14Gb of memory, is the industry's first system to 
deliver performance of more than 300 SPECint92, nearly three times 
the performance  of the next closest competitive system, IBM's SP2.  

     The AlphaServer 8400 system also delivers more than three times 
the maximum SPECrate_int performance of Hewlett-Packard's most 
powerful server, the HP 9000-800 T500 system, and more than twice 
that of Sun's SPARCcenter 2000E system.  It offers twice the I/O 
bandwidth, more than twice the memory capacity, and  more than eight 
times the storage capacity of these competitive commercial SMP 
platforms.

     Available with either Digital UNIX, the first commercially 
available 64-bit enterprise strength UNIX operating system, and 
OpenVMS, Digital's robust, bullet-proof operating environment, 
AlphaServer 8000 systems scale exceptionally well and can be fully 
clustered on either platform, using Digital's unsurpassed clustering 
technology and expertise.

     AlphsServer 8000 systems can meet or exceed the performance of 
specialized massively parallel processing (MPP) systems without the 
risk, complexity or cost associated with MPP systems.  By employing 
familiar, mature SMP, and then clustering multiple SMP systems 
together, large powerful systems can be built that meet the needs of 
today's most demanding applications.  

     The availability of Oracle and other merchant databases and a 
wide selection of application software means customers have many 
options and are not locked into one solution.  These new systems 
today provide customers using commercial parallel processing 
solutions, such as data warehousing, data mining and decision 
support, more cost effective, less complex and less risky options 
powerful enough to do the work that a short time ago only MPP 
systems were thought capable of performing.  

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions, from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace. 
