                  DIGITAL BUILDS ON enVISN MOMENTUM
              BY DETAILING DISTRIBUTED ROUTING STRATEGY


          ...Announces routing options for DECswitch 900...
     ...Introduces RouteAbout family of remote access routers...


MAYNARD, Mass. -- June 26, 1995 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
took the next step in delivering its enVISN network architecture 
today by detailing its distributed routing strategy, which offers 
customers the industry's most cost effective and flexible approach 
to internetworking.  The company outlined products and plans to
distribute routing into hubs, switches, and access devices --
networking products that can be deployed in remote branches and
central sites.  Highlighted in the announcement are routing software
options for the DECswitch 900 department-level switch and the first
members of the new RouteAbout family for branch office networking. 

     Unlike centralized routing schemes requiring costly central 
routers or route servers, the enVISN architecture's distributed 
routing approach distributes the full routing function in multi-
layer switches.  This design puts the "maps and road signs" 
necessary to navigate the data highways at the points where path 
decisions are made, resulting in industry-leading, end-to-end 
network performance and more cost-effective, scalable solutions.  
It is also a step toward the development of One-Hop Switching, 
Digital's unique service that will enable the migration of very high 
performance workgroup virtual LANs (VLANs) into virtual networks.

     "The distributed routing products and plans unveiled today are 
a significant advance toward the virtual network capabilities we 
outlined in our enVISN architecture," said Dr. Laurence G. Walker, 
vice president and general manager of Digital's Network Product 
Business. "Our distributed routing strategy lets customers put 
routing where they need it -- enabling them to build flexible 
networks that can adjust rapidly to support changing business 
needs." 

Distributed Routing Minimizes Network Operating Costs

     The key to distributed routing is the integration of standard 
multiprotocol routing code across hubs, routers, and switches.  This 
approach guarantees complete interoperability across the network, 
and is one way of reducing network operating costs.  Through 
consistent software and easy-to-use network management capabilities 
across the network, distributed routing minimizes training and 
network management costs. 

     For ease of ordering -- and significant cost savings for 
customers supporting several protocols -- the products announced 
today are available in two simple packages: 

     o 	An IP package for bridging and TCP/IP routing; and
     o 	A multiprotocol package that includes the IP package as well 
        as support for the routing IPX, AppleTalk, DECnet, and OSI 
        protocols.  In addition, DLSw support is included for IBM 
        environments.

     All of the products are available as either standalone units or 
as modules that can be placed in a DEChub.  This flexible design 
enables network managers to start small and grow in incremental 
steps as business needs change. 

     "Our recent research on networking configurations for typical 
branch offices and central sites shows that Digital's DEChub-based, 
distributed routing offers customers very cost competitive 
solutions," said Michael Howard, president of Infonetics Research 
Inc., San Jose, Calif. "For example, Digital's solution for a 
central site requiring Ethernet VLANs, an FDDI backbone, and WAN 
links to branch offices is priced 12 percent to 60 percent lower 
than comparable solutions from 3COM, Bay Networks, or Cisco 
Systems."

DECswitch 900 Option Delivers Stage 2 of enVISN 

     Equipped with the new software routing option, the DECswitch 
900 departmental switch delivers the routing between VLANs described 
in Stage 2 of the enVISN rollout.  It is the industry's first switch 
to provide routing between VLANs in the hub.  The routing capability 
can also control network access between traditional shared LANs and 
provide firewalling.  This flexibility gives network managers better 
control over the network, enables them to support more business 
applications, and delivers lower network latency. 

     Announced today are IP and multiprotocol routing software 
options for the DECswitch 900EE (six Ethernet) switch and DECswitch 
900EF (six Ethernet to FDDI) switch.  By the end of the year, a 
DECswitch 900 product to route between Ethernet and Token Ring will 
be added to the family. Prices for these products are as follows:

     Product					Price

     DECswitch 900EE IP Router			$ 7,495
     DECswitch 900EE Multiprotocol Router	$ 8,995
     DECswitch 900EF IP Router			$ 9,495
     DECswitch 900EF Multiprotocol Router	$10,995


Routing can be added to existing DECswitch 900 units through a 
simple software upgrade starting at $1,500.  These products will be 
available in August. 

New RouteAbout Family Connects Remote and Central Sites

     The RouteAbout family gives customers high-speed, flexible ways 
to connect remote branch offices to central sites across a variety 
of WAN services.  The first two members of the family are RouteAbout 
Access EW (Ethernet and 2 T1/E1) for remote sites with Ethernet LANs 
and RouteAbout Access TW (Token Ring and 2 T1/E1) for remote sites 
with Token Ring LANs.  By the end of the year, the RouteAbout family 
will be extended to include a central site router supporting many 
WAN ports and an access product for ISDN environments. 

     The fast-paced Internet market is one example of where the 
RouteAbout products offer a compelling solution.  "With the huge 
growth in Internet access we are experiencing, we need to provide 
remote LAN access quickly and reliably," said Geoff Schultz, 
president of fast growing Internet service provider Ultranet 
Communications Inc., of Marlborough, Mass.  "The RouteAbout products 
are easy to set up and can be configured in minutes.  They provide 
excellent performance for connecting our commercial Internet 
subscribers." 

Beginning prices for these products are as follows:

     Product					      Price

     RouteAbout Access EW with IP routing	      $ 1,950
     RouteAbout Access EW with multiprotocol routing  $ 2,390
     RouteAbout Access TW with IP routing	      $ 2,450
     RouteAbout Access TW with multiprotocol routing  $ 2,890

RouteAbout Access EW products will be available in July; RouteAbout
Access TW products will be available in August. 

enVISN -- The Industry's Most Comprehensive Network Blueprint

     The enVISN blueprint is the industry's most comprehensive, open 
network architecture and product strategy.  It combines VLAN 
technology, distributed routing and high-speed switching with 
centralized, policy-based administration to create flexible virtual 
networks that can be customized to meet application and business 
needs. The enVISN architecture enables businesses to dynamically 
configure flexible, scalable, logical workgroups, whenever and 
wherever they are needed, independent of the physical network
infrastructure. 

     Digital is leading the way in the networking industry today.  
The company is a major player in the "converging" hub, switching, 
and routing marketplace, and is the only networking vendor that 
holds a leadership position in all three elements. The company 
pioneered this convergence model for networking in which these 
technologies are deployed on a single integrated platform -- the 
DEChub 900 MultiSwitch.  Digital's network architecture and 
strategy, enVISN, builds on this platform to enable customers to 
create switched virtual networks. 

     According to revenue figures from International Data 
Corporation (IDC), Dataquest, and Rising Star Research, Digital 
holds the number three market position in routers, hubs, and LAN 
switching.  In addition, Dataquest figures indicate that Digital 
holds the number two market position in Ethernet switching and FDDI.  
Digital has networked more than 12 million users worldwide. 

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated
worldwide information systems. Digital's scalable Alpha platforms,
storage, networking, software, and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace. 
