                DIGITAL SEMICONDUCTOR ANNOUNCES NEW,
            LOW-COST PCI-PCI BRIDGE CHIP FOR PCs, SERVERS


 ...More PCI Expansion Slots With Cost-Effective, Low-Power Chip...


MAYNARD, Mass. -- June 26, 1995 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced a new PCI-to-PCI bridge chip, the second in a 
growing family of bridge products from Digital Semiconductor, a 
Digital Equipment Corporation business.  The new 21052 chip joins 
the 21050, the industry's first PCI-to-PCI bridge chip, which has 
been shipping since October 1994.  The 21052 chip features a very 
competitive price, lower power consumption, and improved clock 
buffering.

     PCI-to-PCI bridge chips enable the addition of expansion slots 
in personal computers, workstations, and servers that incorporate 
the PCI (Peripheral Component Interconnect) bus.  They also allow 
the design of multicomponent and multifunction adapter cards.

     "As a merchant semiconductor vendor, we are continuing our 
track record of being first to market and taking leadership in key 
PCI peripheral areas," said William N. Johnson, vice president, 
Digital Semiconductor Sales and Marketing.  "Just as we use 
innovative products to dominate the 10Mbit and 100Mbit PCI Ethernet 
market, this new chip will consolidate our position in the 
fast-growing PCI-to-PCI bridge market."

     The original PCI-to-PCI bridge chip, the 21050, has been used 
in servers to provide more PCI slots, and also on add-in cards to 
enable the use of more than a single PCI function, such as quad 
Ethernet controllers and dual SCSI cards.  The 21052 chip will 
enable applications in lower-cost systems based on X86, Pentium, 
Alpha, and other processors, as well as lower-cost PCI add-in cards.

     Tracy Richardson, Digital Semiconductor's PCI-to-PCI bridge 
product marketing manager, said, "This new chip is an ideal, 
cost-effective addition for motherboards that require more expansion 
slots than one PCI bus can provide.  The 21052 can also be used to 
develop universal adapter cards, since it supports both 5-volt and 
3.3-volt signaling environments.

     "Unlike competing devices, the 21052 chip has on-chip clock 
buffers, saving both component cost and board space for adapter card 
and system manufacturers," Richardson added.

     "DeskStation Technology plans to use Digital Semiconductor's 
new 21052 PCI-to-PCI bridge chip because of our very positive 
experience with the current 21050 chip.  With PCI-to-PCI bridges, we 
avoid having to create 'hooks' to let users plug third-party cards 
into PCI slots in our systems," said Don Peterson, president, 
DeskStation Technology.  "The chip enables us to add plenty of PCI 
slots quickly, shrinking our time to market and cutting costs.  The 
21052 offers the same advantages as the 21050 with reduced power and 
a lower price."

     The 21052 PCI-to-PCI bridge chip is priced at $16.90 in 
quantities of 5,000 and is available now.

     Digital Semiconductor, a Digital Equipment Corporation business 
headquartered in Hudson, Massachusetts, designs, manufactures and 
markets industry-leading semiconductor products including Alpha 
microprocessors and PCI-based peripheral chips, as well as low-power 
StrongARM microprocessors under license from Advanced RISC Machines 
Ltd.  Digital Semiconductor operates design centers in Hudson;  Palo 
Alto, California;  Austin, Texas;  and Jerusalem, Israel.  
Semiconductor products are manufactured in Hudson and South 
Queensferry, Scotland, and at a new, $450-million fabrication 
facility in Hudson beginning in 1996.  Alpha microprocessors are 
also manufactured under a second-source agreement by Mitsubishi 
Electric Company.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace.
